WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.240
While diehard fans probably know&nbsp;
quite a bit of Sons of Anarchy trivia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.640
there are still untold truths about the&nbsp;
show that are definitely worth discovering.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.440
For fans and anyone else interested, here are&nbsp;
more facts that you may not know about the show.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.840
In the show's first season, Charlie&nbsp;
Hunnam's Jax Teller is introduced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:21.600
as struggling with the pressures of&nbsp;
being the heir apparent of SAMCRO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.560
AKA the Sons of Anarchy Motorcycle Club&nbsp;
Redwood Original. It's something made all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:30.960
the worse for the kid when he stumbles across&nbsp;
some writings by his late father which confirm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.680
Jax's suspicions that the club has become the&nbsp;
opposite of what was originally envisioned.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.480
"Hey. You want me to be your number two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.480
protect this club? Then I gotta know where&nbsp;
you're takin' us, otherwise there's no trust."

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.040
In a 2008 interview, showrunner&nbsp;
Kurt Sutter confirmed this comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.360
directly from the history of the Hells Angels.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:53.360
But to really drill into the tensions&nbsp;
of such a club for seven pulpy seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.800
Sutter also focused on how&nbsp;
motorcycle clubs represent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.440
certain American values while aggressively&nbsp;
rebelling against the establishment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.560
Of course, while they claim to be rebels, they're&nbsp;
not exactly free. As Sutter told The Verge -

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.360
"[Motorcycle clubs] say they're all about 'ride&nbsp;
free' and 'f--- the establishment, but within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.160
the structure of these outlaw clubs, there are&nbsp;
more rules and regulations than you or I have."

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.120
By now the idea of the show being based&nbsp;
on Hamlet has become an ingrained part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.640
of Sons of Anarchy's mythology,&nbsp;
but it isn't exactly correct.

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:29.680
Instead, the play was used to inform Jax's&nbsp;
character and follow his journey into manhood.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.560
However, Sutter has also revealed that Hamlet&nbsp;
served as a tonal reference point for the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:39.360
dramatic arcs, with the play's themes often&nbsp;
overlapping with those of Sons of Anarchy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:44.000
Though the showrunner has admitted he cringes&nbsp;
somewhat when the Shakespearan overtones are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.120
referenced by fans, they're also difficult to&nbsp;
avoid in a show where bodies litter virtually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.320
every episode, and where manipulation,&nbsp;
deception, and hierarchy reign supreme.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.320
If Charlie Hunnam's performance as the&nbsp;
pretty-boy-you-don't-wanna-mess-with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:02.880
biker came across as more authentic than&nbsp;
anyone expected, it's probably because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:08.400
the actor based Jax on the 22 year-old heir&nbsp;
apparent of an Oakland based motorcycle club.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.640
Hunnam spent a lot of time researching&nbsp;
his role and fessed up to The Huffington&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.720
Post that he got to know bikers and club&nbsp;
members really well. It's there that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.280
actor met the young biker who would inform&nbsp;
the complex and tragic character of Jax,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:26.640
right down to the aesthetics, including the&nbsp;
character's clean white kicks and blue jeans.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.640
Hunnam said the guy was perfect for&nbsp;
the character. As the actor explained,

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.760
"His dad was in the club and had&nbsp;
been in the club his whole life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:39.600
And he was 22. He had 22 birthday parties in&nbsp;
the Oakland clubhouse of this motorcycle club.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:43.120
He was the heir apparent, like the&nbsp;
history and future of that club."

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.000
But it came at a cost. The biker who&nbsp;
served as inspiration for Jax was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.560
killed the week after Hunnam left Oakland,&nbsp;
right before the show started shooting. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:55.920
actor still has a necklace from that&nbsp;
time which he wears as a memorial.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.240
With such a commitment to the role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:03.760
it's no surprise Hunnam got so deep in character&nbsp;
that he struggled to leave Jax on set. The British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.440
actor revealed to GQ that he put everything he had&nbsp;
into the show. That includes dressing primarily in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.960
plaid and, oh yes, acting like a legit biker&nbsp;
on and off the screen, with Hunnam saying -

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.160
"I lived it as much as I could. I never got in&nbsp;
a car the whole seven years. I was only on my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.280
bike and rolling around with a bunch of real&nbsp;
bikers and occasionally acting like a maniac."

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:29.760
The line between character and actor was so&nbsp;
blurred that Hunnam even rode a Harley Dyna,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.560
the same bike as Jax. It likely didn't help him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:36.240
to shake off that SAMCRO scuzz when&nbsp;
he went home at the end of the day.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.400
It also made saying goodbye to the&nbsp;
character intensely difficult for Hunnam,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.920
who compared it to feeling, quote,&nbsp;
"like a genuine bereavement."

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.640
Speaking to Glamour about the emotional process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.400
he admitted to returning to the set&nbsp;
after the show had wrapped, saying -

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.640
"I'd just walk around at night because I wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:56.400
be in that environment and go through&nbsp;
a personal process of saying goodbye."

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.880
One of SAMCRO's most interesting&nbsp;
and beloved members is Happy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:05.120
the ironically named sergeant-at-arms of&nbsp;
the motorcycle club who loves killing so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:08.960
much that he tattoos a smiley face&nbsp;
on himself for every life he snuffs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:13.680
out. And the actor behind the character is&nbsp;
about as legit as they come on the show.

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.880
"I'm bringing to the performance&nbsp;
my own personal life experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.880
On some level I've either&nbsp;
seen it happen or lived it."

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:24.560
David Labrava is a former member of&nbsp;
the Hell's Angels who was originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.400
brought on to be a technical advisor.&nbsp;
However, the guy is more than just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:33.040
biker. When Kurt Sutter visited the Oakland&nbsp;
chapter of which Labrava was a member,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.120
he jumped at the opportunity to show off&nbsp;
his creative flair to the showrunner.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.120
Speaking to Collider, Labrava said -

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.280
"I showed him that I wrote scripts, and I asked&nbsp;
him to let me have a chance, when he cast the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:48.240
show. I got cast on the show, and then he gave&nbsp;
me a real chance to write, and here I am."

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:50.800
As well as depicting the trigger-happy biker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:54.320
Labrava also wrote the tenth episode&nbsp;
of the fourth season, "Hands."

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:59.520
Throughout the show's run, Sutter filled&nbsp;
the series with fun, unusual cameos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.000
usually featuring celebrities playing&nbsp;
against type. Season two notoriously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.760
starred anti-fascist punk musician Henry&nbsp;
Rollins as a violent neo-Nazi. Season seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:15.600
featured the infinitely badass Courtney Love as&nbsp;
a sweet-natured pre-K school teacher. Meanwhile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:21.360
wholesome High School Musical star Ashley Tisdale&nbsp;
depicted a high-class escort in season five.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.720
Sutter clearly had great fun with&nbsp;
these playful guest appearances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.800
which were as surprising as they were&nbsp;
oddly fitting for the celebrity involved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.120
Where else could you possibly find David&nbsp;
Hasselhoff playing a retired adult movie star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.840
or revered horror master Stephen King&nbsp;
playing a "cleaner" of dead bodies?

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:39.920
Considering what a gargantuan presence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:45.200
he brought to the show as slick SAMCRO president&nbsp;
Clay Morrow, it may seem utterly inconceivable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:50.880
that Sons of Anarchy could ever exist without&nbsp;
Ron Perlman. But unbelievable though it may be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:55.440
the original pilot actually starred iconic&nbsp;
character actor Scott Glenn in the role.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.360
Speaking to The A.V. Club about his&nbsp;
brief Sons of Anarchy experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:04.240
Glenn actually sounded relieved that he was&nbsp;
fired from the show. The way that Glenn tells it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.960
he found out that FX was going ahead with the&nbsp;
series but without him in the role, and said -

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:12.800
"It was ultimately probably one of the&nbsp;
better things that could've happened to me."

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.280
Enter Perlman, who gave NPR his own side&nbsp;
of the story. Revealing that although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:21.200
he's a fan of Glenn's work, the actor&nbsp;
divulged about Glenn's performance that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:24.800
quote, "the network decided that they&nbsp;
weren't getting what they were hoping."

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:29.760
This required Perlman to take on the role with&nbsp;
hardly any prep time, which might explain why he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:35.520
never exactly became accustomed with riding a hog&nbsp;
the way his fellow Sons of Anarchy co-stars did.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.320
"When I would start the bike, and I would get&nbsp;
ready to pull out at the end of a shot you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:43.280
after all the dialogue was over&nbsp;
people would go 'Cut Cut Cut!'"

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.760
Eager but often mocked, Half-Sack was&nbsp;
a fan–favorite for the two seasons that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.080
the character managed to survive on Sons of&nbsp;
Anarchy, and he likely would've lasted even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:57.840
longer had actor Johnny Lewis not asked Sutter&nbsp;
to cut his time short, with Sutter explaining -

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.240
"Johnny wasn't happy on the show. Creatively,&nbsp;
he really wanted out of his contract."

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:06.800
Just a couple of years after leaving the&nbsp;
series, the 28-year-old was found dead in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.920
a driveway in Los Angeles after allegedly&nbsp;
beating his 81-year-old landlady to death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:16.320
The actor was reportedly struggling with&nbsp;
severe mental health issues at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:19.680
Sutter published a blog post&nbsp;
responding to the actor's death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.400
in which he admitted that he unfortunately&nbsp;
wasn't surprised by the events, and called it -

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.840
"A tragic end for an extremely talented&nbsp;
guy, who had unfortunately lost his way."

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:34.800
Suffice to say, fans were utterly&nbsp;
devastated when Jax's ride-or-die besite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:39.760
Opie, was horrifically killed at the start&nbsp;
of season five. And it seems that the cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:44.640
and crew of the show were hurting just as much&nbsp;
as fans over the lovable character's demise.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.520
To help actor Ryan Hurst and the rest of the cast&nbsp;
properly say goodbye to the character, Hunnam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.560
bought his friend and co-star a samurai sword&nbsp;
and encouraged him to use it to remove his beard.

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:58.960
As Hunnam tells it, the actor hadn't shaved&nbsp;
for the three months since he'd left the show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.320
In fact, he hadn't shaved once&nbsp;
since landing the role of Opie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:05.760
which had been over five years&nbsp;
prior. Hunnam explained -

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.000
"It was a catharsis that we all needed for him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:12.080
to get rid of that beard and for&nbsp;
all of us to just let Opie die."

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:16.640
Cue a supremely emotional video of the&nbsp;
symbolic beard-cutting, in which Hurst,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:21.840
Hunnam, and Mark Boone Junior absolutely sob&nbsp;
as they take turns lopping off Hurst's beard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:26.640
before watching the remainder be shaved off his&nbsp;
face. See? They're big softies at heart, really.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.800
Let's face it. Wearing a whole lot of&nbsp;
leather is one of the least appealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:35.200
options no matter the weather. So to have&nbsp;
to wear such a challenging costume during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.840
long work days on set in 100 degree&nbsp;
heat? Yeah, it's less than ideal.

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:44.560
However, that's exactly the environment that the&nbsp;
Sons of Anarchy cast and crew found themselves in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:49.200
while filming the show. As Kim Coates told&nbsp;
Entertainment Weekly during a set visit -

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:51.920
"We used to call it doing 'the timber.' We'd lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:55.440
a crew member a week from&nbsp;
passing out. Boom. Gone."

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:57.840
Tommy Flanagan, who plays Chibs on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.480
was once in such bad shape from the heat&nbsp;
that he was even sent to hospital for an IV.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:08.480
Super-fans of Sons of Anarchy had the final&nbsp;
episode of their favorite show spoiled due to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:13.760
stupid, throwaway mistake. The snafu was because&nbsp;
of the release of a Sons of Anarchy guide book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:18.320
that was immediately sent out to fans who'd&nbsp;
pre-ordered it. The book arrived just before the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:23.840
final episode, and it basically screamed sensitive&nbsp;
plot points in the faces of unprepared fans.

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:28.800
Kurt Sutter was understandably livid about the&nbsp;
mistake. Despite adding that there were many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:32.960
different people he could blame for the book&nbsp;
being delivered far too early, he also decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:37.360
to shoulder the brunt of the blame for coming up&nbsp;
with the idea for the book in the first place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:42.400
Sutter apologized profusely, and also urged&nbsp;
people not to ruin the ending for others.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.160
Creator and showrunner Kurt Sutter built a bit&nbsp;
of a reputation as a blunt-talking maverick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:51.200
during his days working on&nbsp;
Shawn Ryan's The Shield.

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:56.160
Sutter's reaction to criticisms of the show's&nbsp;
third season, which saw the club head to Ireland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:59.680
could politely be described as&nbsp;
vitriolic. He later called out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:03.840
specific critics who reviewed each episode&nbsp;
in another fiery post on his own blog.

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:09.440
After Sutter's first post-Sons show, The Bastard&nbsp;
Executioner, failed to find an audience and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:14.000
was canceled, he wound up returning to&nbsp;
the world of the Sons with Mayans M.C.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:19.360
a spinoff he co-created Elgin James set&nbsp;
three years after the events of Sons.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:22.160
Mayans M.C. deals with an offshoot of the Mayans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:26.240
who were depicted in Sons of Anarchy as&nbsp;
antagonists and then allies of Charlie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:31.120
Hunnam's Jax Teller. The spinoff deals&nbsp;
with JD Pardo's EZ and his struggles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:35.200
with the Mayans having a different structure&nbsp;
and culture than the Sons. To make the voices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.840
authentic, Sutter was committed to hiring people&nbsp;
of color, telling the Hollywood Reporter -

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:42.640
"I didn't think that it made&nbsp;
creative sense to be the sole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:45.840
voice of a show that takes place&nbsp;
in an entirely different culture."

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:50.720
That attitude probably led to Sutter&nbsp;
being fired from his own co-creation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:56.400
Sutter says Fox's parent company, Disney, disliked&nbsp;
an off-color joke he wrote about Walt Disney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.000
an issue he believes he compounded&nbsp;
by writing more jokes. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.320
the apparent reason for his ouster stemmed&nbsp;
from complaints about his behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:08.720
Sutter moved on and will direct his&nbsp;
feature film debut, Blumhouse's This Beast.

